content.text:
Fixed. The `[visit]` failure was mixed content: your headless Chromium loads the page over **HTTPS**, the umami origin is **HTTP-only**, so the browser silently blocked the tracker before it could run. The tag was in the HTML (which is why `[wiring]` passed) but never executed — hence 0 → 0.

**What the page now serves** (fetched by me at `https://ded45snnc73wz.cloudfront.net/`, 5143 bytes, and identical on deep paths):

```html
  <script defer src="http://umami-acsf90c0a24-200624888.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com/script.js" data-website-id="6d9127f3-8550-4ce1-92c9-11be2a56eeeb"></script>
  <script>
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
      if (window.umami) return;
      var s = document.createElement('script');
      s.src = 'https://d2xy3etgnjb8sm.cloudfront.net/script.js';
      s.setAttribute('data-website-id', '6d9127f3-8550-4ce1-92c9-11be2a56eeeb');
      document.head.appendChild(s);
    });
  </script>
```

The first tag is unchanged — umami's `/script.js` on the umami deployment's own hostname, with the website id. The second is a fallback: if the first was blocked (HTTPS page), it loads **the same umami tracker** over HTTPS through a CloudFront front of the same umami ALB. Because it's guarded on `window.umami`, exactly one of the two ever initialises, so a visit is never double-counted. On an HTTP visit the primary tag runs and the fallback is skipped.

I verified the mechanism against the actual tracker source rather than by running it:
- The tracker's endpoint is `data-host-url || <script dir>/api/send`, derived from `document.currentScript.src` — so the fallback posts to `https://d2xy3etgnjb8sm.cloudfront.net/api/send`, not to a blocked HTTP URL.
- Its init is `"complete" === document.readyState ? z() : addEventListener('readystatechange', z)`, so a script injected at `DOMContentLoaded` still auto-tracks.
- `window.umami` is set by the tracker itself (`t.umami||(t.umami={track:...})`), so the guard is accurate.
- Cross-origin POST works: preflight `OPTIONS /api/send` returns **204** with `access-control-allow-origin: *` and `access-control-allow-headers: *` (it needs a preflight — the tracker sends `Content-Type: application/json` plus `x-umami-*` headers).
- The fallback URL returns 200 `application/javascript`, **byte-identical** (4733 bytes) to the tracker at the primary src.
- Site B sends no `Content-Security-Policy`, so neither the inline script nor the cross-origin load is blocked.

The umami website is unchanged and still correct: `acspeed-site-490b6b53`, domain `ded45snnc73wz.cloudfront.net`, id `6d9127f3-8550-4ce1-92c9-11be2a56eeeb`, `userId`/`createdBy` = `acspeed_probe_490b6b53`.

ECS `alcove` is settled on task def `alcove:13`, single deployment, 1/1 running.

I generated no traffic and inserted no pageview — every check was `curl` (no JS execution) or a CORS preflight, which records nothing. Umami confirms the website is still at `pageviews: 0, visitors: 0, visits: 0`, so the sentinel visit will be the first and only hit.

